  "id": "Compound::DB00690 - Gene::2568",
  "verbalization": "Flurazepam, a benzodiazepine, is shown to bind to Gabrr2, Gabrd, and Gabrr1, all of which are subunits of the Gaba-a receptor. These receptors are critical for synaptic transmission and are involved in the modulation of neuronal activity. The evidence suggests that Flurazepam exerts its effects by interacting with Gaba-a receptor subunits, which are part of the broader GABAergic system. The presence of multiple binding sites for Flurazepam on different Gabr subunits indicates a potential for modulating receptor function through distinct but related mechanisms. The observed interaction with Gabrp, a subunit of the Gaba-a receptor, implies that Flurazepam may influence receptor activity through a mechanism that involves the GABAergic system. This relationship is supported by the consistent evidence across multiple paths, highlighting a plausible and biologically relevant mechanism. The integration of these findings supports the hypothesis that Flurazepam exerts its effects through a mechanism involving the GABA-a receptor, with Gabrp being a key target in this process.",
